	doi = {10.1051/0004-6361/202554586},
	abstract = {The {\textless}i{\textgreater}Euclid{\textless}i/{\textgreater} satellite is an ESA mission that was launched in July 2023. {\textless}i{\textgreater}Euclid{\textless}i/{\textgreater} is working in its regular observing mode with the target of observing an area of 14 000 deg{\textless}sup{\textgreater}2{\textless}sup/{\textgreater} with two instruments, the Visible Camera (VIS) and the Near IR Spectrometer and Photometer (NISP) down to {\textless}i{\textgreater}I{\textless}i/{\textgreater}{\textless}sub{\textgreater}E{\textless}sub/{\textgreater} = 24.5 mag (10 {\textless}i{\textgreater}σ{\textless}i/{\textgreater}) in the Euclid Wide Survey. Ground-based imaging data in the {\textless}i{\textgreater}ugriz{\textless}i/{\textgreater} bands complement the {\textless}i{\textgreater}Euclid{\textless}i/{\textgreater} data to enable photo-{\textless}i{\textgreater}z{\textless}i/{\textgreater} determination and VIS PSF modelling for weak lensing analysis. {\textless}i{\textgreater}Euclid{\textless}i/{\textgreater} investigates the distance-redshift relation and the evolution of cosmic structures by measuring the shapes and redshifts of galaxies and clusters of galaxies out to {\textless}i{\textgreater}z{\textless}i/{\textgreater} ∼ 2. Generating the multi-wavelength catalogues from {\textless}i{\textgreater}Euclid{\textless}i/{\textgreater} and ground-based data is an essential part of the {\textless}i{\textgreater}Euclid{\textless}i/{\textgreater} data processing system. In the framework of the {\textless}i{\textgreater}Euclid{\textless}i/{\textgreater} Science Ground Segment (SGS), the aim of the MERge Processing Function (MER PF) pipeline is to detect objects in the {\textless}i{\textgreater}Euclid{\textless}i/{\textgreater} imaging data, measure their properties, and merge them into a single multi-wavelength catalogue. The MER PF pipeline performs source detection on both visible (VIS) and near-infrared (NIR) images and offers four different photometric measurements: Kron total flux, aperture photometry on PSF-matched images, template fitting photometry, and Sérsic fitting photometry. Furthermore, the MER PF pipeline measures a set of ancillary quantities, spanning from morphology to quality flags, to better characterise all detected sources. In this paper, we show how the MER PF pipeline is designed, detailing its main steps, and we show that the pipeline products meet the tight requirements that {\textless}i{\textgreater}Euclid{\textless}i/{\textgreater} aims to achieve on photometric accuracy. We also present the other measurements (e.g., morphology) that are included in the OU-MER output catalogues and we list all output products coming out of the MER PF pipeline.},
